Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD=2: run a frame ahead, a buffer page of the frame in flight is copied before a guest thread's first write to it, and the GPU thread's buffer reads take the copy until that frame's fence; =1 stays the waiting guard; off by default

T87's guard (=1) made every writer wait for the fence. Batch t87-a: the writers are the game's
job threads, twelve and more of them, writing these pages all through the frame; each waited
20-40 ms a frame and the walk fell from about 18 fps to 12-16. So the wait is the full frame
wait by another road.

=2 keeps the guard's page set and trap, and changes what a trapped write does: under the guard's
lock the page is copied as it is (4 KiB, into a pool of EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD_COPY_PAGES pages,
default 2048), the page's guard and its +1 on the cached count go, and the write goes on. The
buffer cache's reads of guest memory for buffers (mapped uploads, uploads in pieces, the streamed
graphics and compute uniform blocks) go through BufferCache::ReadGuestForBuffer, which takes a
copied page's bytes from its copy, under the same lock, so a read sees a page whole as presented
or from its copy. The small-uniform reuse compares the copy-aware bytes
(BindSmallUniformFromSource) instead of guest memory. At the fence the copies are dropped and
the ranges uploads read from them are handed to the next gather as guest writes
(GPU::GatherAndApply, AheadGuard::TakeRedirty): a buffer uploaded from a copy is then uploaded
again from memory. A page is copied under every device address its memory has. With
the pool full a write waits as with =1. The log line per 300 presents adds pages copied, the
most in one guard, pool-full counts and how much the GPU thread read from copies.

Not covered: texture data and descriptor tables the texture cache reads.

---
